@anon.c8777b812d6b35c09a8e6156f259107b.103 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [2203 x i8] }> <{ [2203 x i8] c"The keys to consider when caching builds for the project.\0A\0ACache keys enable you to specify the files or directories that should trigger a rebuild when modified. By default, uv will rebuild a project whenever the `pyproject.toml`, `setup.py`, or `setup.cfg` files in the project directory are modified, or if a `src` directory is added or removed, i.e.:\0A\0A```toml cache-keys = [{ file = \22pyproject.toml\22 }, { file = \22setup.py\22 }, { file = \22setup.cfg\22 }, { dir = \22src\22 }] ```\0A\0AAs an example: if a project uses dynamic metadata to read its dependencies from a `requirements.txt` file, you can specify `cache-keys = [{ file = \22requirements.txt\22 }, { file = \22pyproject.toml\22 }]` to ensure that the project is rebuilt whenever the `requirements.txt` file is modified (in addition to watching the `pyproject.toml`).\0A\0AGlobs are supported, following the syntax of the [`glob`](https://docs.rs/glob/0.3.1/glob/struct.Pattern.html) crate. For example, to invalidate the cache whenever a `.toml` file in the project directory or any of its subdirectories is modified, you can specify `cache-keys = [{ file = \22**/*.toml\22 }]`. Note that the use of globs can be expensive, as uv may need to walk the filesystem to determine whether any files have changed.\0A\0ACache keys can also include version control information. For example, if a project uses `setuptools_scm` to read its version from a Git commit, you can specify `cache-keys = [{ git = { commit = true }, { file = \22pyproject.toml\22 }]` to include the current Git commit hash in the cache key (in addition to the `pyproject.toml`). Git tags are also supported via `cache-keys = [{ git = { commit = true, tags = true } }]`.\0A\0ACache keys can also include environment variables. For example, if a project relies on `MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET` or other environment variables to determine its behavior, you can specify `cache-keys = [{ env = \22MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET\22 }]` to invalidate the cache whenever the environment variable changes.\0A\0ACache keys only affect the project defined by the `pyproject.toml` in which they're specified (as opposed to, e.g., affecting all members in a workspace), and all paths and globs are interpreted as relative to the project directory." }>, align 1
